{
  "dom_id": "step-q79198",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/d2a8410c92775ef68678a1cac187b3ebc717cfa9fa737ce9723c531616706717.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{\u2033}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{\u2033}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis only at at $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=1.$",
